Variants (MLr_{0}, MLr_{1}, MLv, Mw…) are grouped into families. The original magnitude is not converted or homogenised: the types are not equivalent to one another.
is assigned as follows: “calibrated” if the expected calibration error (ECE) is {0} or less and the slope is between {1} and {2}; “partial calibration” if the ECE is {3} or less; “not calibrated” otherwise; “not evaluated” if there are fewer than {4} positives.
It is compared against the baseline, with data it has not seen and respecting time. With rare events, getting “nothing happens” right is easy: that is why PR-AUC, Brier and calibration are used, not accuracy.
Seven families are compared, from the simplest to the most flexible. No sequential model (recurrent networks) was trained: the number of zone-periods with an event does not justify it.
