If we shuffled the data at random, the model would see aftershocks of the same sequence in training and in test, and would look better than it is. Here the model is always trained on the past and tested on the future, in blocks that advance year by year.
Always with an uncertainty interval.
Seven model families evaluated with the same chronological validation. No model is chosen by accuracy: with rare events, getting “nothing happens” right almost every time does not show usefulness.
It means that in the catalogue that zone has recorded few events above the threshold. It does not guarantee absence of activity: the catalogue covers only a few decades and large earthquakes have much longer recurrence periods.
